messages:
rachel: quick: hit an issue using the JS SDK inside Next middleware (edge). Next 14 canary + SWC. build fails with 'process is not defined' and bundler pulls in node polyfills (fs). minimal: 
```ts
// middleware.ts
import { Client } from 'redwood-inference-js';
export const runtime = 'edge';
export default async (req) => {
  const c = new Client({ apiKey: process.env.RI_KEY });
  await c.generate({ prompt: 'hello' });
  return Response.next();
};
```
miguel: ah yup we still ship some node-only helpers in main entry. edge runtime needs a tiny footprint. what errors do you see from TS?
rachel: types refer to "node:buffer" and ambient Node types so tsc complains about "Cannot find name 'Buffer'" in middleware files. bundler also inlines a small fs shim which fails at runtime.
ankit: two fixes: 1) publish a dedicated edge ESM entry that exports only web-friendly bits; 2) ship a separate .d.ts for that entry with `/// <reference lib=\"webworker\"/>` and no node libs. we did something similar for our browser SDK.
miguel: +1. implementation detail: add an exports map in package.json: 
```json
{
  "exports": {
    "./edge": {"import": "./dist/edge/index.mjs", "types": "./dist/edge/index.d.ts"},
    "./": {"import": "./dist/node/index.mjs", "require": "./dist/node/index.cjs", "types": "./dist/node/index.d.ts"}
  }
}
```
rachel: sounds good. any DX gating we can add so users get a helpful message instead of cryptic bundler errors?
ankit: we can detect common bundler mistakes at runtime/build: if runtime==='edge' and typeof process === 'object' then throw a friendly error with steps: 1) use import from 'redwood-inference-js/edge' or 2) add `/// <reference lib=\"webworker\"/>` to tsconfig or your middleware.d.ts. also add example to examples/next-middleware repo.
miguel: also add an eslint rule in examples to warn when importing default entry in files with `export const runtime = 'edge'`. tiny rule that flags `import .* from 'redwood-inference-js'` in edge files.
ci-bot: note: adding new export affected bundle-snap on linux-canary: +4.2KB. compare at https://ci.internal/changes/1987.
ankit: +4.2KB is fine if it's split export (edge file separate). we'll ensure tree-shaking. also add a test that builds the example with Next canary in CI to catch regressions.
miguel: can we add a one-liner in Quickstart: "Edge runtime users: prefer `import { EdgeClient } from 'redwood-inference-js/edge'` and add webworker lib to TS" so users hit less friction.
rachel: thanks — will open the issue and link a tiny repro. i'll include failing stack trace and `package.json` so devs can reproduce.
ankit: i'll draft PR for exports map + types and add example + eslint rule. assigning to miguel for review.
ci-bot: PR #1922 opened by ankit, status: queued :hourglass: 
miguel: nice. landing this will reduce confusion for Next middleware folks. :party_parrot:
